  "answer": "I don't have a shell tool available, so I'll verify the logic by reasoning through the requirements against the code.\n\nLet me double-check each requirement:\n\n**`DEFAULT_OPTIONS`** — `Object.freeze({ precision: 2, prefix: '', unitLabel: '' })`. Exposes all three defaults and can never be mutated (frozen). ✓\n\n**`resolveOptions(overrides)`**:\n- `undefined` → treated as `{}` (no overrides). ✓\n- `null`, non-object, or array → `TypeError`. ✓\n- Unknown keys → `TypeError` (checked via `Object.keys`, which only sees own enumerable fields). ✓\n- `precision`: must be integer 0–4, else `RangeError`. ✓\n- `prefix`/`unitLabel`: must be strings, else `TypeError`. ✓\n- Returns a **fresh** object each call (new literal), so no shared mutable state. ✓\n- Uses `hasOwnProperty` so only supplied own fields override defaults. ✓\n\n**`formatValue(value, overrides)`**:\n- Only finite numbers accepted, else `TypeError`. ✓\n- Delegates to `resolveOptions`. ✓\n- `formatValue(12.345)` → `\"12.35\"` (prefix `''` + `toFixed(2)`). ✓\n- `{ precision: 1, prefix: '~' }` → `\"~12.3\"`. ✓\n- `{ unitLabel: 'kg' }` → `\"12.30 kg\"`. ✓\n- `{ precision: 1, prefix: '~', unitLabel: 'kg' }` → `\"~12.3 kg\"`. ✓\n- Old call forms work without the new field. ✓\n\nOne subtle point: the spec says \"Reject a non-object, null, or array override with `TypeError`\". My check `typeof overrides !== 'object'` also rejects functions (typeof `'function'`), which is reasonable since a function isn't a plain object. The `Object.keys` unknown-key check handles the rest.\n\nEverything is consistent. Let me confirm the test file expectations match:\n\n- `DEFAULT_OPTIONS` deep-equals `{ precision: 2, prefix: '', unitLabel: '' }` ✓\n- `formatValue(12.345)` === `'12.35'` ✓\n- `formatValue(12.34, { precision: 1, prefix: '~' })` === `'~12.3'` ✓\n- `resolveOptions({ unitLabel: 'kg' })` deep-equals `{ precision: 2, prefix: '', unitLabel: 'kg' }` ✓\n- `formatValue(12.3, { unitLabel: 'kg' })` === `'12.30 kg'` ✓\n\n## Summary\n\nI extended the formatting options across all three modules while preserving the existing exports and behavior.\n\n**`defaults.mjs`**\n- `DEFAULT_OPTIONS` now exposes all three defaults: `{ precision: 2, prefix: '', unitLabel: '' }`, wrapped in `Object.freeze` so it can never be mutated.\n\n**`resolver.mjs`**\n- `resolveOptions(overrides)` now returns a **fresh** object each call (no shared mutable state with `DEFAULT_OPTIONS` or the caller).\n- `undefined` means no overrides; `null`, non-objects, and arrays throw `TypeError`.\n- Unknown keys throw `TypeError` (checked against own enumerable keys).\n- `precision` must be an integer 0–4, otherwise `RangeError`; `prefix` and `unitLabel` must be strings, otherwise `TypeError`.\n- Only supplied own fields override defaults (via `hasOwnProperty`).\n\n**`formatter.mjs`**\n- `formatValue(value, overrides)` rejects non-finite/non-number values with `TypeError`, delegates option resolution to `resolveOptions`, and appends `\" \" + unitLabel` only when `unitLabel` is nonempty.\n- Old call forms still work: `formatValue(12.345)` → `\"12.35\"`, `{ precision: 1, prefix: '~' }` → `\"~12.3\"`, and new `{ unitLabel: 'kg' }` → `\"12.30 kg\"`, `{ precision: 1, prefix: '~', unitLabel: 'kg' }` → `\"~12.3 kg\"`.\n\nNote: I couldn't execute the test runner (no shell tool available), but I verified the implementation against every requirement and the assertions in `public.test.mjs`.",
